t her long eyelashes didn't move at all. [162] \"It's okay if you don't answer. It's not something particularly interesting to me.\" [163] \"What? That explanation still leaves me unsatisfied. I'll tell you anyway. I'm waiting for the first snowfall of the year... Oh, will it snow tomorrow? You're good at predicting the weather, aren't you?\" [164] \"Even if I knew what the weather would be like tomorrow, I couldn't predict if the first snowfall would occur, as there's not enough data. It's better to check the weather forecast in the newspaper, as it's more reliable. And, by the way, there's a fee for my services, so keep that in mind. I make a living from it.\" [165] \"Understood.\" [166] There's nothing for Jingma to investigate. In the future, if life continues, there may be such things, but Jingma has no intention of continuing. [167] The strange girl claimed to be a detective. When Jingma parted with her, the western sky had already begun to turn red. [168] Asking her about the first snowfall was a mistake. After returning to the inn, Jingma felt a little regretful. The clever girl might connect the first snowfall with the legend of this village and uncover Jingma's intentions. [169] Jingma came to the Yinma Tang because he remembered the old story that Kumihira had told him, and it was not the same as the legend of the Dragon's Head. [170] It was a tragic love story in the Edo period. There was a woman in the village who followed the first snowfall for a love affair with a man of different social status and drowned in the Dragon's Pond. As for the content, it was a common and clich\u00e9d story, but what was remarkable was that the phrase \"dying on the day of the first snowfall\" remained in Jingma's heart. [171] Yes, Jirou came to this village to find a place to die. [172] * * *\n[173] Regimen: A straw rope hung in front of a shrine to indicate a prohibition or to bring good luck during the New Year. [174] Water-dried and Uchiwaka: A type of traditional Japanese court garment called \"kimono\". Water-dried and kimono have the same origin, but the former was originally the daily attire of commoners. Unlike kimono, water-dried and kimono are reinforced and connected at the front and back seams with \"chrysanthemum stitching\". Additionally, water-dried and kimono do not have a neck collar like kimono, but are fastened at the neck with a thin belt. Over time, water-dried and kimono became the daily attire of samurai and some members of the imperial court, and eventually became a type of formal wear. Nowadays, female religious figures sometimes wear water-dried and kimono without chrysanthemum stitching. [175] Utsunomiya: Utsunomiya is a Japanese historical figure, the ninth son of Yoshitsune, and a samurai from the Heian period. His childhood name was Utsunomiya, and he was born into the Uesugi clan. [176] [4] Tabi: A type of Japanese short sock, with a divide between the big toe and the other toes, typically made of cotton. Tabi are often worn with Japanese traditional footwear such as geta and zori. [177] 2\n[178] The mother was killed in the middle of October. That evening, Jingma came back from the university and found her mother lying on the floor in the kitchen. She had a knife wound on her chest. Jingma rushed to her and picked her up, but she was already dead, her body as cold as ice. [179] After that, Jingma has little memory of the events. The blood from her mother's wound stained Jingma's sports uniform, and this is the only thing Jingma clearly remembers.\n <Assistant> I have read the book and I will answer the question you ask."